\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

Output-distribution alignment is the primary design dimension, outperforming BCE/MSE across five losses and three architectures (7--14B) with 14.5\% PPL reduction at 30\%.
Gains are task-type dependent---directional commonsense improvements but knowledge regression, structural to KL (C4 control; \S\ref{sec:experiments})---suggesting KL for commonsense/PPL, TEAL for knowledge tasks.
The signal type gap (9.47 PPL) dwarfs coupling breadth (2.08 PPL), though confounded by train--inference mismatch (\S\ref{sec:perlayer}); downstream differences are directional ($n{=}3$, PPL $\sigma{=}0.02$).
Hybrid losses with task-aware allocation are a promising direction. Code released upon acceptance.

